    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 230,
        "endIndex": 329,
        "text": "**What is confirmed**: SpaceX launched Starship Flight 15 from Starbase, Texas, on 13 November 2026"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 331,
        "endIndex": 438,
        "text": "Ship 42 deployed 26 Starlink V3 satellites into Low Earth Orbit and spent approximately 22.5 hours in orbit"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 440,
        "endIndex": 665,
        "text": "On 14 November 2026, Ship 42 re-entered the atmosphere and was successfully caught by the launch tower’s \"chopstick\" arms at Starbase—marking the first time an orbital rocket upper stage has been recovered via tower catch"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 669,
        "endIndex": 828,
        "text": "**Important caveats**: Booster 22 experienced a low-fuel condition and automatically aborted its catch attempt, diverting to a splashdown in the Gulf of Mexico"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 830,
        "endIndex": 925,
        "text": "Thus, the mission achieved a historic upper-stage recovery, but not the targeted \"double catch\""
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 2135,
        "endIndex": 2347,
        "text": "**What is confirmed**: In *Garciaguirre et al. v. Samsung Electronics Co., SK Hynix, & Micron Technology* (U.S. District Court, Northern District of California), the court denied the defendants' motion to dismiss"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        2,
        3
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 2349,
        "endIndex": 2599,
        "text": "The class action alleges that the \"Big Three\" memory makers (controlling ~90% of global DRAM) coordinated legacy DDR3/DDR4 output cuts under the cover of pivoting capacity toward AI-focused High Bandwidth Memory (HBM), driving up consumer DRAM prices"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        3,
        4
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 2603,
        "endIndex": 2790,
        "text": "**Important caveats**: Prior DRAM price-fixing class actions were dismissed by federal courts (e.g., in 2020/2022) due to failure to prove explicit collusion over parallel market behavior"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        5,
        6
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 2792,
        "endIndex": 2848,
        "text": "Plaintiffs still face a high evidentiary burden at trial"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        6
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 3781,
        "endIndex": 4058,
        "text": "**What is confirmed**: *Financial Times* published an op-ed by Chris Miller (*Chip War* author) analyzing Nvidia’s post-Jensen Huang corporate governance and Washington diplomacy following Huang’s resignation on 12 November 2026 (where CFO Colette Kress became interim CEO)"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        7,
        8,
        9
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 4062,
        "endIndex": 4167,
        "text": "**Important caveats**: This is an analytical opinion column rather than a new breaking factual disclosure"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        8
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 4171,
        "endIndex": 4307,
        "text": "**Recommendation**: **SUITABLE FOR PUBLICATION TODAY** as policy/strategy commentary accompanying the wider Nvidia leadership transition"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        8
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 4645,
        "endIndex": 4811,
        "text": "**What is confirmed**: NYC-based startup Nidus Technologies announced $30 million in cumulative pre-seed and seed funding led by Kleiner Perkins and Costanoa Ventures"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        10,
        11
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 4813,
        "endIndex": 4935,
        "text": "Nidus builds AI models for industrial automation robots targeting U.S. defense manufacturing (e.g., wire harness assembly)"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        12,
        13
      ]
    },
    {
      "segment": {
        "startIndex": 4939,
        "endIndex": 5078,
        "text": "**Important caveats**: The $30M represents the combined total raised across pre-seed and seed rounds rather than a single new venture round"
      },
      "groundingChunkIndices": [
        10,
        11
      ]
    },
